WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.268
Assurance for TypeScript application security comes from repeatable habits.

2
00:00:05.268 --> 00:00:12.187
Unit tests can check parsing functions, validation logic, authorization helpers, and error handling.

3
00:00:12.187 --> 00:00:20.286
Integration tests can check API behavior, session-aware routes, database interactions, and framework configuration.

4
00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:26.183
Contract tests can confirm that clients and services agree about validated data shapes.

5
00:00:26.183 --> 00:00:30.822
Security-focused tests should include normal use and likely misuse.

6
00:00:30.822 --> 00:00:46.391
Malformed input, missing fields, overly long values, unsupported enum values, unauthorized access, cross-tenant attempts, partial failures, dependency changes, and important configuration differences all deserve attention.

7
00:00:46.391 --> 00:00:53.860
A test suite that only covers successful UI flows can miss boundary behavior where many security mistakes occur.

8
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:57.006
Code review should look for trust transitions.

9
00:00:57.006 --> 00:01:17.134
Reviewers can ask where external data becomes trusted, whether unknown is parsed before use, whether any or type assertions are justified, whether non-null assertions hide real uncertainty, whether validation happens before sensitive work, and whether errors or logs expose more detail than needed.

10
00:01:17.134 --> 00:01:31.445
Secure delivery is ongoing. CI gates, linting, type checks, dependency checks, static analysis, release notes, environment separation, rollback readiness, and patch processes all help reinforce safe patterns.

11
00:01:31.445 --> 00:01:34.197
The goal is not a one-time security check.

12
00:01:34.197 --> 00:01:43.161
The goal is a development system that keeps validation, authorization, dependency hygiene, and release readiness visible over time.

13
00:01:43.161 --> 00:01:45.677
Review culture is part of that system.

14
00:01:45.677 --> 00:01:49.923
A good review does not only ask whether the TypeScript compiles.

15
00:01:49.923 --> 00:02:00.459
It asks what happens with malformed input, missing session data, unexpected API responses, disabled framework protections, and future dependency updates.

16
00:02:00.459 --> 00:02:06.513
These questions help teams find design issues while they are still small and cheaper to fix.
